{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem he became the Count of Tripoli after the death of his father in 1152. \nThe *Early Life* section of his biography states that Raymond III was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem he was still a minor when his father died and his mother acted as regent until he came of age. \nRaymond III played an important role in the *History of the Crusades* and was a key figure in the Battle of Inab in 1149 and the Battle of Montgisard in 1177. \nThe *Military Campaigns* section of his biography highlights his military campaigns against the Muslim forces led by Saladin and Nur ad-Din he was defeated at the Battle of Homs in 1164 and was forced to pay a large ransom to secure his release. \nRaymond III also played a key role in the *Siege of Jerusalem* in 1187 and was one of the leaders of the Christian forces that defended the city against Saladin's army. \nThe *Personal Life* section of his biography states that Raymond III married Hodierna of Jerusalem but the marriage was childless and he died on September 30 1187 without an heir. \nRaymond III was succeeded by Bohemond IV of Antioch who became the new Count of Tripoli and went on to rule the county for many years. \nThe *Legacy* section of his biography highlights the impact that Raymond III had on the history of the Crusades and the County of Tripoli he was a skilled military leader and played an important role in the defense of the Christian states in the Holy Land. \nRaymond III is also remembered for his role in the *Battle of Hattin* in 1187 where he fought against Saladin's army and was defeated. \nThe *Later Life* section of his biography states that Raymond III spent the last years of his life fighting against Saladin's army an